### My interpretation

Your individual ingredients are not necessarily novel:

- Using LLMs for program repair is established.
- Using compiler feedback is established.
- Using automated Lean tactics is established.
- Restructuring code to make verification easier is established.
- Repairing programs in verification-aware languages is now established.

Your research contribution must therefore be the specific combination and its empirical evidence.

I would position the paper around three contributions:

1. A new repair strategy: Small language models optimize implementation structure for a fixed verifier rather than generating proofs.
2. A controlled empirical study: Establish whether this strategy actually outperforms proof-writing under equivalent resource budgets.
3. A code-shape analysis: Determine which implementation changes make Lean automation succeed, and establish when this strategy does not work.

The third contribution is important. A paper that merely reports that one prompting strategy outperforms another can be relatively weak. A paper that explains why, where, and under what conditions it works is substantially more interesting.

## 3. The biggest problem: novelty is narrower than your proposal currently suggests

After checking the newer literature, I would temper my initial novelty assessment. The idea remains promising, but there is more competition than the original proposal acknowledges.

Two papers deserve particular attention.

P³: Joint Program-and-Proof Planning for Verified Code Generation (August 2026).

This is a very important paper for your project.

Its central observation is that writing an implementation first and worrying about its proof afterwards can lead to implementations that are difficult to verify.

It proposes planning the program and proof together, before generating either.

It also introduces Lean4Commit0, a repository-derived benchmark built from 108 real-world open-source libraries. Its evaluation reports improvements of 4.6–11.2 percentage points over its stronger baseline across different benchmarks and model backends.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://arxiv.org\&sz=32)

arXiv+1



Why this matters to you: The general idea that implementation structure should anticipate verification is no longer a new observation.

However, P³ uses frontier models and model-generated proof scaffolds. Your approach deliberately delegates proof generation to a fixed automation pipeline and targets resource-constrained coding models.

That distinction needs to become central to your paper.

RefactorProof: How Stable Are LLM-Generated Proofs Under Program Refactoring? (October 26, 2026).

This is another particularly relevant development.

The authors construct 458 Lean-certified program variants from 185 VERINA tasks and investigate whether changing program structure while preserving semantics affects the validity of existing proofs.&#x20;

[image](https://www.google.com/s2/favicons?domain=https://arxiv.org\&sz=32)

arXiv



Why this matters to you: It establishes that code structure and proof behaviour are worth studying directly in Lean, using VERINA itself.

Your contribution needs to go beyond demonstrating that code restructuring affects proof success.

It should establish whether a small coding model can learn to perform useful verification-directed repairs, whether this is better than model-written proofs, and which transformations account for the improvement.

There is also a recent ASE 2026 APR-track paper, DafnyFix, which repairs faulty Dafny programs using deterministic transformation heuristics and reports 62.5% repair success. This means automated repair in verification-aware programming languages is another area where you need a careful distinction.&#x20;

