### What should count as success?

I would retain your strict definition:

A repair counts as successful only when:

- The implementation compiles.
- The unchanged formal contract has a valid proof.
- The additional behavioural tests pass.
- No forbidden axioms, proof escapes or integrity violations occur.
- No specification, test or verification-harness modification occurs.

The paper should distinguish these outcomes rather than reporting only a single success percentage.

This is one of the strongest aspects of the existing proposal.

## 6. Is it manageable as a three-person group project?

Yes. In fact, I particularly like the team structure in your second PDF.

The three components have relatively clear boundaries:

| Member | Responsibility                          | Main deliverable                                        |
| ------ | --------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| A      | Lean verification engine                | Tactic pipeline, verifier, integrity checker            |
| B      | Dataset and models                      | Fault injection, model serving, prompts, generation     |
| C      | Research and experimental orchestration | Literature review, experiment driver, statistics, paper |

The division is sensible because each person owns one stage of the research pipeline, and the interfaces are limited to a task manifest and an experiment log.&#x20;

AutoSE_Project_Plan.pdf



### What I would watch out for

Member A is the biggest technical risk.

Lean 4, Mathlib, tactic orchestration, theorem checking, integrity checking and reproducible builds are not trivial engineering tasks.

If this component is unreliable, every experimental result becomes questionable.

I would therefore require the following before the main study:

- A clean Lean build on another machine.
- Successful verification of all selected reference tasks.
- Unit tests for every integrity check.
- Identical verifier results across repeated runs.
- Proper timeout and crash handling.

Member B has the second biggest risk.

Fault injection sounds easy, but producing mutants that compile, are genuinely incorrect, and can be repaired without leaking reference solutions is harder than it looks.

Every mutant should have a documented provenance, fault category, failing witness and verified correct reference.

Member C should not be treated as merely the report writer.

The experimental design, preregistration, statistical methodology and novelty assessment are central research contributions. This person should be involved in deciding the experiment before anyone starts collecting results.

