## 8. What would make this an A\*-quality research paper?

I would establish three milestones.

Milestone 1

A credible workshop publication

Complete the experiment, demonstrate a reproducible B-versus-C comparison, publish the repair harness and provide a useful failure analysis.

A clear negative result is also acceptable scientifically.

Milestone 2

A strong main-track SE paper

Add the controlled proofability study, naturally generated faulty programs, stronger statistical evidence, and independent validation beyond the initial VERINA mutation set.

Establish exactly when the strategy succeeds, not merely its overall average improvement.

Milestone 3

An ambitious A submission\*

Develop a more general method for automation-directed program repair, evaluate it on larger and more realistic Lean programs, demonstrate robustness across models and task distributions, and show that the findings generalize beyond one fixed tactic portfolio.

This would be a substantially stronger contribution than a prompting comparison.

One thing I especially like about your project plan is that it already has backup studies if the central hypothesis fails:

- Characterizing how far fixed Lean automation can go.
- Building a proof-friendliness benchmark.
- Studying proof repair across model sizes.

These are worthwhile alternative research directions, provided the experiments are sufficiently rigorous.&#x20;

